238	Use a Choice when the answer is one of a fixed set of options. For example, which team handles a ticket, which category a product belongs to, or which language a code snippet is written in. If the answer is a position on a spectrum, use a [Score](/primitives/score). If it's a yes or no, use a [Noul](/primitives/noul). [Choose a question type](/primitives#choose-a-question-type) compares all three.
239	
240	A Choice answer is the selected option in `choice`. The model also returns a probability for every option in `probabilities`, and a `confidence` value for the selected option.
241	
242	Example questions:
243	
244	```
245	"What programming language is this code written in"
246	  → options: python, javascript, typescript, go, rust, other
247	
248	"What type of meeting is this based on the title and description"
249	  → options: standup, planning, retrospective, one on one, brainstorm, none of the above
250	
251	"Which product category does this item belong to"
252	  → options: electronics, clothing, home garden, food and beverage
253	```
254	
255	## Request structure
256	
257	The POST request body to the [TypeSafe API](/api) has a specific structure. The top level has three fields: `state`, the content to evaluate; `model`; and `questions`, a map from question ids you choose to question objects. Each Choice question has the following fields:
258	
259	* `type`: Always `"choice"`.
260	* `instructions`: The question the model answers.
261	* `criteria`: The answer options, as a map. Each key is an option name and each value is a description of that option.
262	
263	Below is a request where the state is a support ticket from an online shoe store and the question is which team should handle it:
264	
265	<TypesafeExample
266	  display="request"
267	  example={{
268	state: 'My running shoes arrived in the wrong size. Can I swap them for a size 10?',
269	selectedModels: ['jev-latest'],
270	questions: {
271	  department: {
272	    type: 'choice',
273	    instructions: 'Which team should handle this?',
274	    criteria: {
275	      returns: 'Exchanges, wrong or damaged items',
276	      shipping: 'Delivery status, delays, lost packages',
277	      billing: 'Charges, invoices, payment problems',
278	    },
279	  },
280	},
281	}}
282	/>
283	
284	You choose the question id, `department` in this case. The answer is returned under the same id. The model never sees the question id. The option names and their descriptions are both sent to the model, so write descriptions that separate the options from each other.
285	
286	Our [client SDKs](/sdk) provide typed questions. In Python, the same question is a `Choice`:
287	
288	```python theme={null}
289	from typesafe_sdk import Choice, TypeSafeClient
290	
291	with TypeSafeClient() as client:
292	    response = client.system_one(
293	        state="My running shoes arrived in the wrong size. Can I swap them for a size 10?",
294	        questions={
295	            "department": Choice(
296	                instructions="Which team should handle this?",
297	                criteria={
298	                    "returns": "Exchanges, wrong or damaged items",
299	                    "shipping": "Delivery status, delays, lost packages",
300	                    "billing": "Charges, invoices, payment problems",
301	                },
302	            ),
303	        },
304	    )
305	
306	    print(response.answers["department"].choice)
307	```
308	
309	Use the `system_one` method or the `https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone` endpoint to call a System One model. The `model` field selects which model handles the request. [How to build with TypeSafe](/concepts/how-to-build-with-system-one) covers where in your code to call it.
310	
311	Use one of our [client SDKs](/sdk) or call the [HTTP API](/api) directly. If a coding agent is writing the integration for you, install the [TypeSafe agent skill](/agent-skill#installation) first so it knows the request and response shapes.
312	
313	<Note>
314	  `instructions` and each entry in `criteria` can be a string, an object, or an array. Start with a string. Use an object when a description needs several kinds of guidance, such as what an option covers, what it doesn't cover, and some examples. See [Structured instructions and criteria](#structured-instructions-and-criteria) below and the [API reference](/api#param-instructions-1).
315	</Note>
316	
317	## Response structure
318	
319	The response has one entry in `answers` per question, under the ids from the request. This is the response to the example request above:
320	
321	```json theme={null}
322	{
323	  "model": "jev-1.13.0",
324	  "answers": {
325	    "department": {
326	      "type": "choice",
327	      "choice": "returns",
328	      "confidence": 1.0,
329	      "probabilities": {
330	        "shipping": 0.0,
331	        "returns": 1.0,
332	        "billing": 0.0
333	      }
334	    }
335	  },
336	  "usage": {
337	    "input_tokens": 328,
338	    "output_tokens": 34
339	  }
340	}
341	```
342	
343	Besides `type`, each Choice answer has three values:
344	
345	* `choice`: The option with the highest probability.
346	* `probabilities`: The full probability distribution across every option. The sum of all values is 1.
347	* [`confidence`](/confidence): A number from 0 to 1 computed from how `probabilities` is spread. A flat shape, with probability spread across several options, means low confidence. A single peak on one option means high confidence.
348	
349	This ticket is an easy one, so all of the probability is on `returns` and confidence is 1.0. A ticket that mentions a wrong size and a missing refund would split probability between `returns` and `billing`, and confidence would drop.
350	
351	## Good practice: ask more than one question per call
352	
353	Ask every Choice question your code might need in a single request rather than one request per question. Questions are evaluated in parallel. Adding questions barely changes the response time, and the code can ignore answers it doesn't need. Extra questions still cost tokens. [Ask multiple questions together](/primitives#ask-multiple-questions-together) explains this in full; the next section shows five Choice questions in one call.
354	
355	The same logic applies to the options inside a single Choice question. A Choice question accepts up to 255 options, and adding options costs a few tokens each, so give the model the full list of teams, categories, or products rather than a shortlist. Add an `other` or `none of the above` option when the list might not cover every input, so the model can say none of the others fit.
356	
357	To classify documents through a deep hierarchy or large taxonomy, chain Choice questions level by level. The [Hierarchical Classification cookbook](/cookbooks/hierarchical_classification) shows how to run a beam search over Choice probabilities, keeping the best `K` candidate paths at each level instead of committing to a single greedy path.
358	
359	## A more complex example
360	
361	The basic example above routes a ticket to a team. A bigger support system might also need the return reason, the delivery problem, what the customer wants, and the customer's tone.
362	
363	The request below asks five Choice questions about a ticket that is more ambiguous than the first: it involves three teams and doesn't say what the customer wants.
364	
365	<TypesafeExample
366	  display="request"
367	  example={{
368	state: 'Shoes arrived two weeks late and in the wrong size. Also I see two charges of $120 on my card. What are you going to do about this?',
369	selectedModels: ['jev-latest'],
370	questions: {
371	  department: {
372	    type: 'choice',
373	    instructions: 'Which team should handle this?',
374	    criteria: {
375	      returns: 'Exchanges, wrong or damaged items',
376	      shipping: 'Delivery status, delays, lost packages',
377	      billing: 'Charges, invoices, payment problems',
378	    },
379	  },
380	  return_reason: {
381	    type: 'choice',
